2094	In SO4^-2 the oxidation number of Sulphur is	-8	+8	-6	+6	D	medium	In SO4²⁻, each oxygen is −2 (total −8); for the ion's overall charge to be −2, sulphur must be +6.	Let x be sulphur's oxidation number. With 4 oxygens at −2 each: x + 4(−2) = −2, so x − 8 = −2, giving x = +6.	Set up the algebra: (oxidation numbers) sum to the ion's overall charge — oxygen is almost always −2.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.48954+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.720778+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.489739+05	12	181	t	f	\N	{"a": "This would make the overall charge far more negative than −2.", "b": "This would make the overall charge positive, not −2.", "c": "This doesn't balance the equation x − 8 = −2.", "d": "Correct — x − 8 = −2 gives x = +6."}
