1966	During successions, the first organisms that develop on bare rock are:	Lichens	Moss	Shrubs	Herbs	A	medium	Lichens are the pioneer organisms that first colonize bare rock during ecological succession, breaking it down to eventually form soil.	Lichens (a symbiosis of fungi and algae/cyanobacteria) can survive on bare rock with minimal nutrients, slowly breaking down the rock surface and contributing organic matter — making them the classic pioneer species in primary succession on rock.	Bare rock succession always starts with lichens — the classic pioneer species.	2026-08-23 17:06:57.083275+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.264775+05	2026-08-23 17:06:57.083375+05	11	126	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — lichens are the textbook pioneer organisms in primary succession on bare rock.", "b": "Moss typically colonizes after lichens have begun breaking down the rock and creating some soil.", "c": "Shrubs come much later in succession, once sufficient soil has developed.", "d": "Herbs establish after mosses, once more soil and organic matter are present — not as the first colonizers."}
1985	Those nephrons which are present along the border of the cortex and medulla are called:	Juxtamedullary nephrons	Internal nephrons	Cortical nephrons	Outer nephrons	A	medium	Nephrons located along the border between the cortex and medulla, with long loops of Henle extending deep into the medulla, are called juxtamedullary nephrons.	Juxtamedullary nephrons (named for being "next to the medulla") have long loops of Henle that penetrate deep into the renal medulla, playing a key role in producing concentrated urine via the countercurrent multiplier mechanism.	"Juxta-medullary" = next to the medulla — nephrons positioned at the cortex-medulla border with long loops.	2026-08-23 17:06:57.122965+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.378404+05	2026-08-23 17:06:57.123049+05	11	218	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — juxtamedullary nephrons are specifically those positioned along the cortex-medulla border.", "b": "\\"Internal nephrons\\" isn't the standard anatomical term used for this classification.", "c": "Cortical nephrons are located mainly within the cortex, with shorter loops, not specifically at the cortex-medulla border.", "d": "\\"Outer nephrons\\" isn't the standard anatomical term used for this classification."}
2057	At triple point of water, the pressure of gas is 2680 Pa; by changing 'T' the pressure increases to 4870 Pa. Then 'T' is:	496.38 K	438.96 K	Zero	496.38 °F	A	medium	Using the constant-volume gas law P/T = constant with the water triple point (273.16 K, 2680 Pa) as reference: T = 273.16 × (4870/2680) ≈ 496.4 K.	At constant volume, pressure and temperature are directly proportional (P/T = constant). Taking the triple point of water (273.16 K at 2680 Pa) as the reference point and scaling up to 4870 Pa gives T ≈ 496.4 K, matching option A.	Constant-volume gas law: P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂ — scale from the known triple point.	2026-08-18 16:08:29.136825+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.562084+05	2026-08-18 16:08:29.136965+05	12	202	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — matches 273.16 × (4870/2680).", "b": "Doesn't match the proportional scaling from the triple point.", "c": "Zero isn't physically meaningful for a real pressure increase.", "d": "Same numeric value as A but in the wrong unit — this needs kelvin, not Fahrenheit."}
2059	A heat engine working according to second law of thermodynamics has 50% efficiency. What will be the temperature of its low temperature reservoir if high temperature reservoir is 327°C?	27°C	127°C	300°C	600°C	A	medium	Efficiency = 1 − Tc/Th; with 50% efficiency and Th = 600 K (327°C + 273), Tc = 300 K = 27°C.	Carnot efficiency is 1 − (Tc/Th) using absolute temperatures. Converting 327°C to 600 K and solving for 50% efficiency gives Tc = 300 K, which converts back to 27°C.	Always convert to Kelvin first for efficiency = 1 − Tc/Th.	2026-08-18 16:08:29.143577+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.56866+05	2026-08-18 16:08:29.143705+05	12	202	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — Tc = 0.5 × 600 K = 300 K = 27°C.", "b": "Doesn't satisfy 50% efficiency with Th = 600 K.", "c": "Doesn't satisfy the efficiency formula for Th = 600 K.", "d": "This is higher than Th's Celsius value, which can't be the cooler reservoir."}
2040	Two forces, 5 N and 10 N are acting at 'O' and 'P' respectively on a uniform meter rod suspended at the position of centre of gravity 50 cm mark, as shown in the figure. What is the position of 'P' on meter rod?	80 cm	75 cm	70 cm	65 cm	B	medium	Balancing clockwise and anticlockwise moments about the 50 cm pivot (using the diagram's given distance for O) places P at 75 cm.	For equilibrium, the sum of clockwise moments must equal the sum of anticlockwise moments about the pivot. Using O's distance from the pivot and the 5 N and 10 N forces, solving gives P at the 75 cm mark, as shown in the original figure.	Principle of moments: force × distance from pivot must balance on both sides.	2026-08-18 16:08:29.001228+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.475486+05	2026-08-18 16:08:29.001438+05	12	193	t	t	\N	{"a": "Doesn't satisfy moment balance for the given forces and O's position.", "b": "Correct — matches the moment balance for 5 N at O and 10 N at P.", "c": "Distance too small; moments wouldn't balance.", "d": "Distance too small; moments wouldn't balance."}
2042	Which of the following is the best graphical representation between drag force 'F' on a spherical object of radius 'r' and its speed 'v' through a fluid of viscosity 'η'?	Graph A	Graph B	Graph C	Graph D	A	medium	For slow, viscous (laminar) flow, drag force is directly proportional to speed (Stokes' law) — a straight line through the origin, graph A.	Stokes' law states F = 6πηrv, meaning drag force increases linearly with speed for a small sphere moving slowly through a viscous fluid. This is represented by a straight line graph passing through the origin.	Stokes' law: F ∝ v — a straight line, not a curve.	2026-08-18 16:08:29.011072+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.50352+05	2026-08-18 16:08:29.011326+05	12	195	t	t	\N	{"a": "Correct — matches the linear F ∝ v relationship of Stokes' law.", "b": "A curved graph doesn't match Stokes' law for laminar flow.", "c": "Doesn't represent the direct proportionality between F and v.", "d": "Doesn't represent the direct proportionality between F and v."}
2043	What is the speed of an incompressible non-viscous liquid flowing out from 'B' contained in a container as shown in the figure? Where AB = 5 m and g = 10 m/s2.	5 m/s	10 m/s	2 m/s	50 m/s	B	medium	Using Torricelli's law v = √(2gh) with h = 5 m and g = 10 m/s², v = √100 = 10 m/s.	For an incompressible, non-viscous fluid escaping from a small opening at depth h below the surface, Bernoulli's equation reduces to Torricelli's law, v = √(2gh). Substituting h = 5 m and g = 10 m/s² gives v = 10 m/s.	Torricelli's law: v = √(2gh) — same as free-fall speed from height h.	2026-08-18 16:08:29.016637+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.506325+05	2026-08-18 16:08:29.016854+05	12	194	t	t	\N	{"a": "Too small — doesn't satisfy √(2×10×5).", "b": "Correct — √(2×10×5) = √100 = 10 m/s.", "c": "Too small for the given height and g.", "d": "Too large — √(2gh) with these values gives 10, not 50."}
