2079	A radioactive isotope 'W' decays to 'X' (β-decay), 'X' decays to 'Y' (α-decay), and 'Y' decays to 'Z' (α-decay). What is the change in the atomic number from 'W' to 'Z'?	Increases by 3	Decreases by 3	Increases by 5	Decreases by 5	B	medium	One β-decay (+1) followed by two α-decays (−2 each) gives a net change of +1−2−2 = −3.	Tracking atomic number through each step: β-decay increases Z by 1, and each of the two subsequent α-decays decreases Z by 2. Adding these changes: +1 −2 −2 = −3, so the atomic number decreases by 3 overall.	Add up each step's Z change: β = +1, α = −2 — total them for the full chain.	2026-08-18 16:08:29.235367+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.649968+05	2026-08-18 16:08:29.235709+05	12	313	t	f	\N	{"a": "Doesn't match summing +1, −2, and −2 across the three decay steps.", "b": "Correct — +1 (β) −2 (α) −2 (α) = −3 net change.", "c": "Doesn't match the correct sum of the three decay steps' Z changes.", "d": "Doesn't match the correct sum of the three decay steps' Z changes."}
