2076	A beta (β) particle is a fast-moving electron. During a β-decay how do the atomic number and mass number of a nucleus change?	Atomic Number remains the same, Mass Number increases by one	Atomic Number increases by one, Mass Number decreases by two	Atomic Number increases by one, Mass Number remains the same	Atomic Number decreases by two, Mass Number decreases by four	C	medium	In beta decay, a neutron converts to a proton and an electron, so atomic number increases by 1 while mass number stays the same.	Beta decay emits an electron from the nucleus when a neutron transforms into a proton. This adds one proton (atomic number +1) but doesn't change the total nucleon count, so mass number is unchanged.	Beta decay: atomic number +1, mass number unchanged.	2026-08-18 16:08:29.216433+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.641515+05	2026-08-18 16:08:29.21658+05	12	313	t	f	\N	{"a": "Mass number doesn't change in beta decay — this is incorrect.", "b": "Mass number stays the same in beta decay, it doesn't decrease.", "c": "Correct — atomic number +1, mass number unchanged, as in beta decay.", "d": "This describes two alpha decays, not a single beta decay."}
2078	A naturally occurring radioactive element decays by emitting two alpha particles. Which one of the following represents the status of daughter element with respect to mass number 'A' and charge number 'Z'?	'Z' decreases by 4 and 'A' decreases by 2	'Z' decreases by 2 and 'A' decreases by 4	'Z' decreases by 4 and 'A' decreases by 8	'Z' decreases by 8 and 'A' decreases by 4	C	medium	Each alpha particle removes 2 protons and 4 nucleons; two alpha decays together reduce Z by 4 and A by 8.	An alpha particle is a helium nucleus (2 protons, 2 neutrons). Emitting two of them removes a total of 4 protons (Z decreases by 4) and 8 nucleons (A decreases by 8) from the parent nucleus.	One α-decay: Z−2, A−4. Two α-decays: just double it — Z−4, A−8.	2026-08-18 16:08:29.227706+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.647255+05	2026-08-18 16:08:29.228168+05	12	313	t	f	\N	{"a": "This mismatches Z and A changes — doesn't reflect two α-decays correctly.", "b": "This is the change for only ONE alpha decay, not two.", "c": "Correct — two α-decays reduce Z by 4 and A by 8.", "d": "Swaps and inflates the changes incorrectly — doesn't match two α-decays."}
2112	Addition of unsymmetrical reagent to an unsymmetrical alkene is governed by:	Cannizzaro's Reaction	Kirchhoff Rule	Aldol Condensation	Markownikov's Rule	D	medium	Markownikov's Rule governs how an unsymmetrical reagent (like HX) adds across an unsymmetrical alkene's double bond.	Markownikov's Rule states that in the addition of HX to an unsymmetrical alkene, the hydrogen attaches to the carbon that already has more hydrogens, while X attaches to the more substituted carbon — this reflects the more stable carbocation intermediate formed.	'Unsymmetrical reagent + unsymmetrical alkene' is the classic cue phrase for Markownikov's Rule.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.594732+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.805161+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.594916+05	12	163	t	f	\N	{"a": "Cannizzaro's Reaction concerns aldehydes without alpha-hydrogens undergoing disproportionation, unrelated to alkene addition.", "b": "Kirchhoff's Rule is a thermodynamics/physics law about heat capacity, not organic addition reactions.", "c": "Aldol Condensation involves carbonyl compounds forming new C-C bonds, not alkene addition.", "d": "Correct — Markownikov's Rule governs unsymmetrical addition to unsymmetrical alkenes."}
2140	It is our national duty to ________________ our vote in the general election.	Throw	Cast	Drop	Refuse	B	medium	The standard collocation is 'cast a/our vote,' not throw, drop, or refuse a vote.	In English, 'vote' pairs idiomatically with the verb 'cast' — 'cast a vote' is the fixed expression used for the act of voting, making 'Cast' the only natural fit here.	Vote is always 'cast,' never 'thrown' or 'dropped' — memorize this fixed collocation.	2026-08-18 16:22:35.418879+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.92251+05	2026-08-18 16:22:35.419398+05	12	287	t	f	\N	{"a": "'Throw a vote' isn't standard English usage.", "b": "Correct — 'cast our vote' is the fixed, idiomatic collocation.", "c": "'Drop a vote' isn't standard English usage.", "d": "'Refuse' contradicts the sense of duty in the sentence (refusing isn't casting)."}
