2082	A polymer of empirical formula CH2 has molar mass of 28000 g mol-1. Its molecular formula will be	100 times that of its empirical formula	200 times that of its empirical formula	500 times that of its empirical formula	2000 times that of its empirical formula	D	medium	Molecular mass ÷ empirical mass = 28000 ÷ 14 = 2000, so the molecular formula is 2000 times the empirical formula.	The empirical formula CH2 has a mass of (12 + 2×1) = 14 g/mol. Dividing the given molar mass (28000 g/mol) by this empirical mass gives 2000, meaning the molecular formula is (CH2)2000.	n = Molecular mass ÷ Empirical formula mass — always divide, don't multiply.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.363176+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.662962+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.363801+05	12	321	t	f	\N	{"a": "100× would need a molar mass of 1400 g/mol, not 28000.", "b": "200× would need a molar mass of 2800 g/mol, not 28000.", "c": "500× would need a molar mass of 7000 g/mol, not 28000.", "d": "Correct — 28000 ÷ 14 = 2000."}
2083	The number of molecules in 9 g of ice (H2O) is	6.02 x 10^24	6.02 x 10^23	3.01 x 10^24	3.01 x 10^23	D	medium	9 g of ice is 0.5 mol H2O, so it contains 0.5 × 6.02×10²³ = 3.01×10²³ molecules.	Moles = mass ÷ molar mass = 9 g ÷ 18 g/mol = 0.5 mol. Multiplying by Avogadro's number (6.02×10²³ /mol) gives 3.01×10²³ molecules.	Always convert mass to moles first, then multiply by Avogadro's number — never skip the mole step.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.425908+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.668367+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.426123+05	12	235	t	f	\N	{"a": "This would be for 18 g (1 mole), not 9 g.", "b": "This is Avogadro's number itself, not the molecule count for 0.5 mol.", "c": "Off by a factor of 10 from the correct value.", "d": "Correct — 0.5 mol × 6.02×10²³ = 3.01×10²³ molecules."}
2084	Ice is less dense than water at:	0°C	4°C	-4°C	2°C	A	medium	Ice is less dense than liquid water right at 0°C, where it floats on the water it just formed from.	Water has its maximum density at 4°C. As it freezes at 0°C, the open hydrogen-bonded lattice of ice makes it less dense than the liquid water at that same temperature, which is why ice floats.	Water is densest at 4°C, not at 0°C — ice is always less dense than liquid water.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.432205+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.674472+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.432459+05	12	162	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — at 0°C, ice (solid) is less dense than the liquid water around it.", "b": "4°C is water's maximum density point, not related to ice formation.", "c": "-4°C is below freezing but the density comparison is made at the melting point, 0°C.", "d": "2°C is still liquid water territory, not the ice/water density comparison point."}
2085	At a given temperature and pressure, the one which shows marked deviation from ideal behavior is	N2	N3	CO2	He	C	medium	CO2 is a larger, more polarizable molecule than the other gases listed, so it deviates most from ideal behavior.	Ideal gas behavior assumes negligible intermolecular forces and molecular volume. CO2, being larger and more polarizable than N2 or He, experiences stronger van der Waals forces, causing the greatest deviation from ideal gas law predictions.	Bigger, more polarizable molecules (like CO2) deviate more from ideal gas behavior than small, light ones (like He).	2026-08-18 16:20:04.439975+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.680262+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.440176+05	12	239	t	f	\N	{"a": "N2 is a small, nonpolar diatomic molecule with weak intermolecular forces — close to ideal.", "b": "N3 (azide) isn't a stable common gas being compared here.", "c": "Correct — CO2's larger size and polarizability cause the greatest deviation.", "d": "He is the smallest, least polarizable gas — behaves most ideally."}
2086	According to the number of protons, neutrons and electrons given in the table (As: 33p/42n/30e, Ga: 31p/39n/28e, Ca: 20p/20n/20e), which one of the following options is correct?	As-3, Ga+3, Ca	As+1, Ga+2, Ca	As+3, Ga+3, Ca+2	As+1, Ga, Ca+2	C	medium	Charge = protons − electrons: As (33p/30e) = +3, Ga (31p/28e) = +3, Ca (20p/20e) = neutral.	Comparing protons to electrons for each species: arsenic has 3 more protons than electrons (+3 charge), gallium has 3 more protons than electrons (+3 charge), and calcium has equal protons and electrons, making it neutral.	Charge = (number of protons) − (number of electrons); more protons than electrons means a positive ion.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.445128+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.685611+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.445323+05	12	176	t	f	\N	{"a": "As-3 would mean more electrons than protons, but As has fewer electrons — wrong sign.", "b": "As+1 and Ga+2 don't match the proton-electron differences given.", "c": "Correct — As+3, Ga+3, and neutral Ca all match the proton-electron counts.", "d": "Ca+2 is wrong since Ca has equal protons and electrons (neutral)."}
