2056	Which feature of the following graph represents Young's Modulus? (Strain on the vertical axis, Stress on the horizontal axis)	Area under graph	Gradient of the graph	Reciprocal of the gradient	Product of gradient and area of the curve	C	medium	Since this graph plots strain (y-axis) against stress (x-axis) — the reverse of usual — Young's modulus (stress/strain) equals the reciprocal of the gradient.	Young's modulus is defined as stress ÷ strain. If the axes are swapped so strain is vertical and stress horizontal, the graph's gradient gives strain/stress instead, which is 1/E. So E itself is the reciprocal of this gradient.	If axes are swapped from the usual stress(y)/strain(x), take the reciprocal of the gradient.	2026-08-18 16:08:29.133718+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.556839+05	2026-08-18 16:08:29.133847+05	12	262	t	t	\N	{"a": "The area under this graph doesn't represent Young's modulus.", "b": "The gradient here is strain/stress (1/E), not E itself, since the axes are swapped.", "c": "Correct — because axes are swapped, E = 1/gradient.", "d": "Multiplying gradient and area doesn't correspond to Young's modulus."}
