2115	If halogenoalkanes are mixed with an excess of ethanoic ammonia and heated under pressure, amines are formed. Which amine is formed in the reaction CH3CH2Br + NH3 → Amine?	CH3-CH2-NH-CH2-CH3	CH3-CH2-NH2	CH3-CH2-CH2-NH2	H2N-CH2-CH2-NH2	B	medium	CH3CH2Br reacting with ammonia via nucleophilic substitution replaces Br with NH2, giving the primary amine CH3-CH2-NH2 (ethylamine).	Ammonia's lone pair attacks the carbon bearing the leaving bromide in an SN2 reaction, displacing Br⁻ and forming ethylamine (CH3CH2NH2) as the primary product when NH3 is in excess.	Alkyl halide + NH3 (excess) → primary amine, via simple substitution of the halogen by -NH2.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.611994+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.821627+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.612179+05	12	253	t	f	\N	{"a": "This secondary amine would form from further reaction of the primary amine with more alkyl halide, not the direct single product.", "b": "Correct — direct substitution of Br by NH2 gives ethylamine.", "c": "This has an extra CH2, not matching the two-carbon ethyl bromide starting material.", "d": "This is a diamine, requiring a dihalide starting material, not CH3CH2Br."}
2116	Primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols can be identified and distinguished by	Lucas test	Iodoform test	Baeyer's test	Silver mirror test	A	medium	The Lucas test uses the differing reaction rates of 1°, 2°, and 3° alcohols with ZnCl2/HCl to distinguish between them.	In the Lucas test, tertiary alcohols react immediately (instant cloudiness), secondary alcohols react within a few minutes, and primary alcohols show little to no reaction at room temperature — this rate difference identifies the alcohol type.	Lucas test speed: tertiary = instant, secondary = a few minutes, primary = very slow/no reaction.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.619558+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.828246+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.619752+05	12	257	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — the Lucas test distinguishes alcohol classes by their differing reaction rates.", "b": "The Iodoform test identifies specific methyl-carbinol structural patterns, not general 1°/2°/3° classification.", "c": "Baeyer's test detects unsaturation (double/triple bonds), not alcohol classification.", "d": "The silver mirror test (Tollens') detects aldehydes, not alcohol type."}
2117	Which one of the following alcohols is indicated by formation of yellow crystals in Iodoform test?	Methanol	Ethanol	Butanol	Propanol	B	medium	Ethanol gives a positive Iodoform test (yellow precipitate of CHI3) because it has the required CH3-CH(OH)- structural pattern.	The Iodoform test is positive for compounds containing a methyl ketone or a secondary alcohol with a methyl group on the carbinol carbon (CH3-CH(OH)-R). Among the options, only ethanol fits this pattern, producing a yellow crystalline precipitate of iodoform.	Iodoform-positive alcohols must have the CH3-CH(OH)- fragment; methanol lacks it, and larger alcohols may not have it either depending on structure.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.624571+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.831844+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.624774+05	12	257	t	f	\N	{"a": "Methanol lacks the required CH3-CH(OH)- fragment, so it doesn't give a positive test.", "b": "Correct — ethanol's CH3-CH(OH)- structure gives a positive Iodoform test.", "c": "Depending on its structure, butanol may lack the necessary CH3-CH(OH)- fragment for a positive test here.", "d": "Propanol (n-propanol) doesn't have the CH3-CH(OH)- pattern needed for a positive Iodoform test."}
2118	Ethyl butyrate and butyl butanoate are esters with the flavor of	Pear	Banana	Pineapple	Apple	C	medium	Ethyl butyrate and butyl butanoate are esters known for smelling like pineapple.	Different esters are associated with distinct fruity smells based on their structure; ethyl butyrate in particular is a well-known compound responsible for a strong pineapple-like aroma, often used in flavorings.	Ethyl butyrate = classic pineapple-smell ester used in flavor chemistry questions.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.629609+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.834604+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.629852+05	12	377	t	f	\N	{"a": "Pear aroma is typically associated with esters like propyl acetate, not butyrate esters.", "b": "Banana aroma is typically linked to isoamyl acetate, not ethyl/butyl butyrate.", "c": "Correct — these butyrate esters are associated with a pineapple flavor.", "d": "Apple aroma is typically linked to esters like methyl butyrate variants used differently, not this specific pairing."}
2120	Which one of the following groups is indicated when HCl is formed by reaction of ethanol with phosphorous pentachloride?	Amino group	Hydroxyl group	Halide group	Hydride group	B	medium	HCl gas evolving when ethanol reacts with PCl5 confirms the presence of a hydroxyl (-OH) group, which is replaced by chlorine.	PCl5 reacts specifically with -OH groups, converting them to -Cl and releasing HCl and POCl3 as byproducts. The evolution of HCl gas (detected as white fumes with ammonia) is a classic test confirming an alcohol's hydroxyl group.	PCl5 + HCl fumes evolved = confirms a hydroxyl (-OH) group is present in the compound tested.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.642679+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.840848+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.643001+05	12	253	t	f	\N	{"a": "PCl5's characteristic HCl-releasing reaction targets -OH groups, not amino groups.", "b": "Correct — the reaction with PCl5 specifically indicates a hydroxyl group.", "c": "A halide group wouldn't react with PCl5 to release HCl in this way; it's already halogenated.", "d": "A hydride group isn't the functional group being tested for or converted here."}
2121	A student mixed ethyl alcohol with a small amount of sodium dichromate and added it to a hot solution of dilute sulphuric acid. A vigorous reaction took place. He distilled the product formed immediately. What was the product?	Acetone	Acetic acid	Dimethyl ether	Acetaldehyde	D	medium	Mild oxidation of ethanol with sodium dichromate/dilute sulphuric acid, followed by immediate distillation, gives acetaldehyde before it can be further oxidized.	Sodium dichromate in acidic conditions oxidizes primary alcohols first to aldehydes; distilling the product off immediately removes the volatile acetaldehyde from the reaction mixture before it can be oxidized further to acetic acid.	Immediate distillation after mild oxidation of a primary alcohol = stops at the aldehyde stage (acetaldehyde), not the acid.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.649984+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.84354+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.650203+05	12	377	t	f	\N	{"a": "Acetone would come from oxidizing a secondary alcohol (like isopropanol), not ethanol.", "b": "Acetic acid would form only with excess oxidant and no immediate removal/distillation — not this careful procedure.", "c": "Dimethyl ether isn't produced by oxidation of ethanol under these conditions.", "d": "Correct — mild, controlled oxidation with immediate distillation yields acetaldehyde."}
2122	The structural formula of the product of reaction of acetone with 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine is:	Structure A	Structure B	Structure C	Structure D	D	medium	Acetone reacts with 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine to form the corresponding 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazone, a classic carbonyl-detection derivative.	The -NH2 of 2,4-DNP condenses with the carbonyl carbon of acetone, losing water and forming a C=N-NH-(2,4-dinitrophenyl) linkage — a bright orange/yellow crystalline hydrazone derivative used to identify and characterize ketones and aldehydes.	Carbonyl compound + 2,4-DNP → hydrazone derivative (C=N-NH-Ar) with loss of water — a standard carbonyl identification test.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.655911+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.847468+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.656175+05	12	353	t	t	\N	{"a": "Doesn't show the correct hydrazone linkage formed between acetone's carbonyl and 2,4-DNP.", "b": "Doesn't correctly represent the condensation product structure.", "c": "Doesn't correctly represent the condensation product structure.", "d": "Correct — shows acetone's carbonyl carbon condensed with 2,4-DNP to form the hydrazone."}
2125	Organic compounds 'X' and 'Y' both can react with Na-Metal to evolve hydrogen gas. If 'X' and 'Y' react with each other to form an organic compound 'Z' which gives fruity smell, what type of compounds are 'X', 'Y' and 'Z'?	X: Alcohol, Y: Ester, Z: Acetic Acid	X: Alcohol, Y: Ester, Z: Mineral Acid	X: Alcohol, Y: Acetic Acid, Z: Ester	X: Alcohol, Y: Mineral Acid, Z: Ester	C	medium	X and Y both react with Na to release H2 (so both have -OH/-COOH groups); X is an alcohol and Y is acetic acid, which combine to form ester Z with a fruity smell.	Both alcohols and carboxylic acids react with sodium metal to liberate hydrogen gas. When an alcohol (X) reacts with a carboxylic acid like acetic acid (Y) in an esterification reaction, the fruity-smelling ester Z is formed, along with water.	Fruity smell + reaction of an acid and alcohol = esterification, producing an ester (Z).	2026-08-18 16:20:04.676694+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.861379+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.676912+05	12	150	t	f	\N	{"a": "This reverses X, Y, Z roles — the product Z should be an ester, not acetic acid.", "b": "A mineral acid (like HCl) wouldn't react with Na to release H2 in the same organic-reaction context, nor combine to form a fruity ester this way.", "c": "Correct — alcohol (X) + acetic acid (Y) → ester (Z) with fruity smell.", "d": "A mineral acid doesn't fit the described reaction producing a fruity organic ester."}
2126	The amino acids which are not prepared in human body are called	Essential amino acids	Non-essential amino acids	Alpha amino acids	Beta amino acids	A	medium	Essential amino acids cannot be synthesized by the human body and must be obtained through diet.	Unlike non-essential amino acids, which the body can synthesize from other metabolic intermediates, essential amino acids (like leucine, lysine, etc.) must come from external food sources because the body lacks the enzymatic pathways to make them.	'Essential' = 'must get from food' — the body cannot make these amino acids itself.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.684605+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.864124+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.685058+05	12	150	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — essential amino acids must be obtained from the diet since the body can't synthesize them.", "b": "Non-essential amino acids are, by definition, the ones the body CAN make on its own.", "c": "Alpha amino acids refers to the general structural class (amino and carboxyl on the same carbon), not to dietary necessity.", "d": "Beta amino acids refers to a different structural arrangement, unrelated to whether they're synthesized by the body."}
2128	Which one of the following is Glutamic Acid? (four amino-acid structures)	Structure A	Structure B	Structure C	Structure D	A	medium	Glutamic acid's structure has two carboxyl (-COOH) groups and one amino (-NH2) group on a five-carbon backbone (HOOC-CH2-CH2-CH(NH2)-COOH).	As an acidic amino acid, glutamic acid carries an extra carboxylic acid group on its side chain in addition to the standard alpha-carboxyl and alpha-amino groups — giving it the structure shown correctly as option A.	Glutamic acid = 'acidic' amino acid with an extra -COOH on its side chain (two COOH total).	2026-08-18 16:20:04.700661+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.869301+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.700887+05	12	252	t	t	\N	{"a": "Correct — shows two -COOH groups and one -NH2 group, matching glutamic acid's structure.", "b": "Shows an incorrect number/placement of functional groups for glutamic acid.", "c": "Shows an incorrect number/placement of functional groups for glutamic acid.", "d": "Shows an incorrect number/placement of functional groups for glutamic acid."}
2129	At low pH or in acidic condition amino acid exists as	Anion	Cation	Zwitter ion	Neutral specie	B	medium	In acidic conditions, the amino group of an amino acid picks up an extra proton, giving the molecule an overall positive charge (a cation).	At low pH, excess H+ ions protonate the -NH2 group to -NH3+, while the -COOH group remains largely un-ionized, so the amino acid carries a net positive charge and exists predominantly as a cation.	Low pH (acidic) = amino acid becomes a cation (+); high pH (basic) = amino acid becomes an anion (−).	2026-08-18 16:20:04.708587+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.874959+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.709061+05	12	148	t	f	\N	{"a": "An anion (negative charge) forms in basic, not acidic, conditions.", "b": "Correct — protonation of -NH2 to -NH3+ under acidic conditions gives a net positive charge (cation).", "c": "The zwitterion (neutral overall, with both + and − charges) exists near neutral pH, not low pH.", "d": "A fully neutral species doesn't describe the protonated state present at low pH."}
2130	The structure shown below (partially recovered: CH=C-CH2-CH(NH2)-COOH with an imidazole-like N/NH ring) represents:	Proline	Histidine	Glycine	Lysine	B	medium	The structure with an imidazole-type ring (containing two nitrogens) in its side chain identifies the amino acid as histidine.	Histidine is unique among standard amino acids for having an imidazole ring side chain, which contains two nitrogen atoms and gives histidine its characteristic weakly basic, buffering properties.	Imidazole ring side chain = histidine, the amino acid with buffering/basic properties near physiological pH.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.716269+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.878965+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.716571+05	12	302	t	t	\N	{"a": "Proline has a pyrrolidine ring involving the backbone nitrogen, not a separate imidazole side chain.", "b": "Correct — the imidazole-containing side chain identifies histidine.", "c": "Glycine has the simplest possible side chain (just a hydrogen atom), no ring at all.", "d": "Lysine has a straight-chain side chain ending in an -NH2 group, no ring."}
2131	Which one of the following reagents is used for identification of amino acids?	Fehling's solution	Benedict's solution	Ninhydrin	Copper (II) Sulphate	C	medium	Ninhydrin is the classic reagent used to detect and identify amino acids, producing a purple (Ruhemann's purple) color.	Ninhydrin reacts with the free amino group of amino acids to form a deep purple/blue colored complex (Ruhemann's purple), making it the standard test for detecting amino acids, peptides, and proteins.	Ninhydrin test → purple color → confirms presence of free amino acids.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.722884+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.881895+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.723156+05	12	148	t	f	\N	{"a": "Fehling's solution tests for reducing sugars, not amino acids.", "b": "Benedict's solution also tests for reducing sugars, not amino acids.", "c": "Correct — Ninhydrin specifically identifies amino acids via a purple color reaction.", "d": "Copper (II) sulphate alone (without other reagents) doesn't specifically test for amino acids."}
2132	Which one of the following is an example of condensation polymer?	Polyvinylchloride	Polystyrene	Polyethene	Polyamide	D	medium	Polyamide (e.g., nylon) is a condensation polymer, formed with the loss of small molecules like water during polymerization.	Condensation polymers form when monomers join with the elimination of a small molecule (often water); polyamides like nylon form via amide (peptide-like) bonds between diamine and diacid monomers, releasing water at each linkage.	Condensation polymers lose a small molecule (usually H2O) at each bond; addition polymers (like polyethene, polystyrene, PVC) don't.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.730579+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.887956+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.730886+05	12	168	t	f	\N	{"a": "Polyvinylchloride is an addition polymer, formed by simple monomer addition with no byproduct.", "b": "Polystyrene is also an addition polymer, made by joining styrene monomers without losing any small molecule.", "c": "Polyethene is the simplest addition polymer, formed from ethene monomers with no byproduct.", "d": "Correct — polyamide forms via condensation, releasing water at each amide linkage."}
