2134	Fats are a type of lipid called glycerides. They are esters of long chain carboxylic acids with:	Propene-1,2,3-triol	Propane-1,2,3-triol	Propene-1,2,3-diol	Propane-1,2,3-diol	B	medium	Fats (glycerides) are esters formed from long-chain carboxylic (fatty) acids combined with glycerol, chemically named propane-1,2,3-triol.	Glycerol has three -OH groups on a saturated three-carbon chain, giving it the systematic name propane-1,2,3-triol. Each -OH can esterify with a fatty acid, and when all three do, a triglyceride (fat) is formed.	Glycerol = propane-1,2,3-triol (note: 'propane' since it's saturated, not 'propene').	2026-08-18 16:20:04.747945+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.894842+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.748283+05	12	334	t	f	\N	{"a": "Uses 'propene,' implying a double bond, but glycerol is fully saturated (propane-based).", "b": "Correct — glycerol is chemically propane-1,2,3-triol.", "c": "Uses 'propene' (unsaturated) and 'diol' (only two -OH groups) — both incorrect for glycerol.", "d": "Uses 'diol' (only two -OH groups), but glycerol has three hydroxyl groups (a triol)."}
