2139	Which one of the following pollutants can cause death of a person by binding with haemoglobin of red blood cells?	Chlorofluorocarbons	Oxides of Sulphur	Carbon monoxide	Oxides of nitrogen	C	medium	Carbon monoxide binds tightly to haemoglobin (forming carboxyhaemoglobin), blocking oxygen transport and causing death.	CO binds to the iron in haemoglobin about 200 times more strongly than oxygen does, forming stable carboxyhaemoglobin. This prevents oxygen from being carried to tissues, leading to asphyxiation and potentially death — the basis of CO poisoning.	CO poisoning = CO out-competes O2 for haemoglobin binding sites, cutting off oxygen delivery to the body.	2026-08-18 16:20:04.780515+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.913904+05	2026-08-18 16:20:04.780844+05	12	334	t	f	\N	{"a": "Chlorofluorocarbons deplete the ozone layer; they don't cause death by binding haemoglobin.", "b": "Oxides of sulphur cause respiratory irritation and acid rain, not haemoglobin binding.", "c": "Correct — carbon monoxide binds haemoglobin, blocking oxygen transport and causing death.", "d": "Oxides of nitrogen contribute to smog and acid rain, not haemoglobin binding in this way."}
2145	SPOT THE ERROR: “(All is well)[a] (what ends well)[b],” (said the father)[c] (when he had finished the story.)[d]	All is well	what ends well	said the father	when he had finished the story.	B	medium	The well-known proverb is 'All's well that ends well,' so 'what ends well' is the error — it should be 'that ends well.'	This is a famous English proverb (also a Shakespeare play title) with a fixed wording using the relative pronoun 'that,' not 'what.' Substituting 'what' breaks the correct, established phrasing.	Fixed proverbs and idioms must be quoted exactly — 'All's well THAT ends well,' never 'what.'	2026-08-18 16:22:35.479737+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:47.944537+05	2026-08-18 16:22:35.48+05	12	208	t	f	\N	{"a": "'All is well' is an acceptable variant of the proverb's opening.", "b": "Correct — the error; should be 'that ends well,' not 'what ends well.'", "c": "'Said the father' is a correct reporting clause.", "d": "'When he had finished the story' is grammatically correct."}
