2233	T-lymphocytes recognize antigen and attack microorganisms or transplanted organ and tissues. This effect is called	Cell-mediated response	Humoral immune response	Active immunity	Passive immunity	A	medium	T-lymphocytes directly attacking pathogens/foreign tissue is called cell-mediated response.	When T-lymphocytes directly recognize and destroy infected cells or foreign tissue (like transplants) without antibody involvement, this is termed the cell-mediated immune response, as opposed to the antibody-based humoral response.	T-cells = cell-mediated immunity; B-cells/antibodies = humoral immunity.	2026-08-18 16:28:27.677393+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:48.279273+05	2026-08-18 16:28:27.677557+05	12	128	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — direct T-lymphocyte attack on antigens/cells is the cell-mediated response.", "b": "Humoral immune response involves antibodies produced by B-cells, not direct T-cell attack.", "c": "Active immunity is a broader category (body producing its own response), not the specific term for this T-cell mechanism.", "d": "Passive immunity involves receiving pre-made antibodies, unrelated to this direct T-cell action."}
2234	Which part of antibody recognizes the antigen during immune response?	Heavy part	Light part	Constant part	Variable part	D	medium	The variable region of an antibody is responsible for recognizing specific antigens.	Each antibody's variable region (at the tips of the heavy and light chains) has a unique shape that binds specifically to its target antigen, while the constant region mediates other immune functions.	Variable region = antigen recognition (unique per antibody); constant region = same across antibody class.	2026-08-18 16:28:27.682517+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:48.281692+05	2026-08-18 16:28:27.682714+05	12	128	t	f	\N	{"a": "The heavy chain includes both variable and constant portions; it's the variable part specifically that recognizes antigen.", "b": "The light chain also includes variable and constant portions; again it's the variable part that matters for recognition.", "c": "The constant region is uniform and doesn't vary to recognize different antigens.", "d": "Correct — the variable part of the antibody recognizes the specific antigen."}
2235	What type of immunity is achieved by injecting antibodies, antiserum, anti-venom serum?	Active immunity	Passive immunity	Artificially induced immunity	Naturally induced immunity	B	medium	Injecting pre-formed antibodies (antiserum) confers passive immunity.	Passive immunity is achieved when ready-made antibodies (from antiserum, anti-venom, or maternal transfer) are directly introduced into the body, providing immediate but temporary protection, unlike active immunity which involves the body producing its own antibodies.	Injected antibodies = passive immunity; body-produced antibodies (via infection/vaccine) = active immunity.	2026-08-18 16:28:27.686748+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:48.284579+05	2026-08-18 16:28:27.686923+05	12	128	t	f	\N	{"a": "Active immunity involves the body making its own antibodies, not receiving pre-made ones.", "b": "Correct — injecting pre-formed antibodies/antiserum confers passive immunity.", "c": "'Artificially induced immunity' is too broad/vague; the precise term for injected antibodies is passive immunity.", "d": "'Naturally induced immunity' refers to immunity gained through natural infection, not injection."}
