2238	Oxidative phase of glycolysis starts with dehydrogenation of	Glycolysis	Ribulose Bisphosphate	Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate	NADH	C	medium	The oxidative phase of glycolysis begins with dehydrogenation of glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate.	In glycolysis, the energy-yielding (oxidative) phase starts when glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate is oxidized (dehydrogenated) to 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate, generating NADH — a key step distinguishing the energy-investment and energy-payoff phases.	G3P dehydrogenation marks the start of the payoff/oxidative phase of glycolysis, producing NADH.	2026-08-18 16:28:27.701414+05	t	2026-08-12 01:54:48.297342+05	2026-08-18 16:28:27.701684+05	12	121	t	f	\N	{"a": "'Glycolysis' itself is the whole pathway, not the specific molecule dehydrogenated at this step.", "b": "Ribulose bisphosphate is involved in the Calvin cycle (photosynthesis), not glycolysis.", "c": "Correct — glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate is dehydrogenated to start the oxidative phase.", "d": "NADH is the product of this dehydrogenation step, not the substrate being dehydrogenated."}
