1818	One method of creating an inverted population is known as _________ and consist of illuminating the laser material with light.	Optical Pumping	Bremsstrahlung	Excitation	Holography	A	medium	Population inversion is created by optical pumping, illuminating the laser material with light so more atoms sit in the excited state than the ground state.	Optical pumping means shining light of the right energy onto the laser medium so atoms absorb photons and jump to a higher energy level. Done fast enough, more atoms end up excited than in the ground state — a population inversion, the condition needed for laser action.	"Pump" = push atoms up with light. Optical pumping = light does the pushing.	2026-08-23 16:59:39.452217+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:02.001632+05	2026-08-23 16:59:39.452488+05	11	370	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — optical pumping is exactly the process of illuminating the laser material with light to create population inversion.", "b": "Bremsstrahlung is X-ray radiation emitted when fast electrons decelerate near a nucleus — unrelated to laser pumping.", "c": "Excitation is the general result (an atom moving to a higher state), not the specific illumination technique used to achieve it.", "d": "Holography is a 3D imaging technique using laser light, not a method of creating population inversion."}
1819	In population inversion (Ruby Laser) atoms can reside in the excited state for:	10-11	10-3	10-8	10+3	B	medium	In a ruby laser, atoms sit in the metastable excited state for about 10⁻³ seconds before decaying — far longer than an ordinary excited state.	Normal excited states decay almost instantly (around 10⁻⁸ s). The metastable state used in a ruby laser is special because atoms linger there for roughly a thousandth of a second, giving time for population inversion to build up before stimulated emission occurs.	Metastable = "stays a while." Milliseconds (10⁻³ s), not nanoseconds.	2026-08-23 16:59:39.489122+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:02.051279+05	2026-08-23 16:59:39.489212+05	11	313	t	f	\N	{"a": "10⁻¹¹ s is far too short — shorter even than a normal excited-state lifetime.", "b": "Correct — the metastable state's long ~10⁻³ s lifetime is what makes population inversion possible.", "c": "10⁻⁸ s is the typical lifetime of an ordinary (non-metastable) excited state, not the long-lived one used here.", "d": "10⁺³ s (about 17 minutes) is far too long for any atomic excited state."}
