1825	During the decay of radioactive isotopes 90X to a stable isotope, six α-particles and four β- particles are emitted, what is the atomic number ‘Z’ and mass number ‘A’ of the stable isotopes.	Z = 70, A = 220	Z = 82, A = 212	Z = 78, A = 212	Z = 82, A = 208	D	medium	Six alpha particles reduce Z by 12 and A by 24; four beta particles then increase Z by 4 with no further change to A, giving Z = 90−12+4 = 82 and A = 232−24 = 208.	Starting nuclide has Z=90 (implied by ₉₀X). Each alpha removes 2 protons and 4 mass units: 6 alphas remove 12 protons and 24 mass units. Each beta emission converts a neutron to a proton, raising Z by 1 without changing A: 4 betas add 4 to Z. Net: Z = 90 − 12 + 4 = 82, and A = 232 − 24 = 208 (using the natural parent mass number 232, consistent with a thorium-232 decay series).	Alpha: Z−2, A−4. Beta: Z+1, A unchanged. Tally them separately.	2026-08-23 16:59:39.505903+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:02.091872+05	2026-08-23 16:59:39.505962+05	11	313	t	f	\N	{"a": "Z=70 doesn't match 90−12+4=82; this option miscounts the proton changes.", "b": "A=212 doesn't match the mass-number arithmetic (232−24=208) for six alpha emissions.", "c": "Z=78 undercounts the +4 contribution from the four beta emissions.", "d": "Correct — Z = 82 and A = 208 follow from six alpha (−12, −24) and four beta (+4, 0) emissions starting from A=232, Z=90."}
