2009	From evolutionary point of view, which respiratory protein is common in many organisms?	Cytochrome a	Cytochrome c	Cytochrome b	Cytochrome d	C	medium	Cytochrome c is highly conserved across many species from an evolutionary standpoint, making it a classic molecule used to study evolutionary relationships.	Cytochrome c's amino acid sequence has been compared across countless organisms because it's essential for cellular respiration and has changed relatively little over evolutionary time, making it a valuable molecular clock for tracing evolutionary relationships.	Cytochrome c = the classic "evolutionary molecule" used in comparative biochemistry studies.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.701661+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.539307+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.701756+05	11	126	t	f	\N	{"a": "Cytochrome a is part of the electron transport chain but isn't the specific molecule famous for comparative evolutionary studies.", "b": "Correct — cytochrome c is the well-known respiratory protein used to trace evolutionary relationships across species.", "c": "Cytochrome b is part of the electron transport chain but isn't the specific molecule famous for this evolutionary use.", "d": "Cytochrome d isn't the standard molecule referenced in this evolutionary comparison context."}
2010	Number of pairs of autosomes in humans in:	23	21	24	22	D	medium	Humans have 22 pairs of autosomes (non-sex chromosomes), plus one pair of sex chromosomes, totaling 23 pairs (46 chromosomes).	Of the 23 total chromosome pairs in humans, 22 pairs are autosomes (numbered 1-22), and the 23rd pair consists of the sex chromosomes (XX or XY) — a key distinction in human genetics.	23 total pairs = 22 autosome pairs + 1 sex chromosome pair.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.703392+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.544831+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.70346+05	11	132	t	f	\N	{"a": "23 is the total number of chromosome pairs (including sex chromosomes), not just the autosome count.", "b": "21 undercounts the standard number of human autosome pairs.", "c": "24 overcounts the standard number of human autosome pairs.", "d": "Correct — 22 pairs of autosomes, plus 1 pair of sex chromosomes, make up the human genome's 23 total pairs."}
2011	ABO blood system is an example of:	Polygenes	Multiple Alleles	Multiple genes	Multiple Mutation	C	medium	The ABO blood group system is a classic example of multiple alleles, where more than two allele variants (A, B, and O) exist for a single gene locus, though the answer key marks "Multiple genes" for this listing.	The ABO blood type gene has three common alleles (IA, IB, i), more than the two alleles typical of simple Mendelian traits — the textbook-correct example of multiple allelism, though this listing's answer key marks a different classification.	ABO blood type = classic multiple alleles example (three alleles: A, B, O, at one gene locus).	2026-08-23 17:09:25.704902+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.549463+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.70496+05	11	132	t	f	\N	{"a": "Polygenes involve multiple different genes affecting one trait, not multiple alleles at a single locus like ABO.", "b": "\\"Multiple alleles\\" is indeed the textbook-correct classification for ABO blood type, though the answer key marks a different option here.", "c": "Marked correct per the answer key for this listing.", "d": "\\"Multiple mutation\\" isn't a standard genetics classification term matching this concept."}
2012	Which molecular structure of enzyme is essential for activity of enzyme?	Primary Structure	Secondary Structure	Quaternary Structure	Tertiary Structure	D	medium	An enzyme's tertiary structure — its overall 3D folded shape, including the precisely formed active site — is essential for its catalytic activity.	While primary structure (amino acid sequence) underlies everything, it's the tertiary structure (the complete 3D folding pattern) that creates the specific active site shape needed for substrate binding and catalysis — making it essential for enzyme function.	Enzyme activity depends on tertiary structure — the 3D fold that creates the functional active site.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.70637+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.55897+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.706428+05	11	291	t	f	\N	{"a": "Primary structure (amino acid sequence) is necessary but not sufficient alone — folding into 3D shape is what creates function.", "b": "Secondary structure (local folding patterns like alpha-helices) contributes to but doesn't alone determine full enzyme activity.", "c": "Quaternary structure applies only to multi-subunit enzymes, not a universal requirement for all enzyme activity.", "d": "Correct — tertiary structure (the full 3D fold, including the active site) is essential for enzyme activity."}
2015	Which organelle is bounded by two membranes?	Ribosome	Lysosome	Mitochondria	Nucleolus	B	medium	Lysosomes are bounded by a single membrane, while structures like mitochondria and the nucleus are bounded by double membranes — the answer key marks lysosome as double-membraned for this specific listing.	Standard cell biology classifies lysosomes as single-membrane-bound organelles (unlike mitochondria, chloroplasts, and the nucleus, which have double membranes) — though this listing's answer key marks lysosome as the double-membraned option.	Double-membraned organelles: nucleus, mitochondria, chloroplasts. Single-membraned: lysosomes, vacuoles, ER, Golgi.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.711038+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.572852+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.711094+05	11	117	t	f	\N	{"a": "Ribosomes have no membrane at all — they're not membrane-bound organelles.", "b": "Marked correct per the answer key for this listing, though lysosomes are standardly classified as single-membrane organelles.", "c": "Mitochondria are indeed double-membraned in standard classification, though not the option marked in the key here.", "d": "The nucleolus is a non-membrane-bound structure within the nucleus, not itself double-membraned."}
2016	At the beginning of nuclear division, the number of microtubule triplets in two pairs of centrioles that migrate to opposite poles are:	9	108	18	36	D	medium	At the start of nuclear division, two pairs of centrioles migrate to opposite poles; with each centriole containing 9 microtubule triplets, two pairs (4 centrioles total) contain 4×9=36 triplets.	Each individual centriole has 9 microtubule triplets. Since centrioles exist in pairs, and two pairs (4 centrioles total) migrate to the opposite poles of the dividing cell, the total triplet count is 4 × 9 = 36.	1 centriole = 9 triplets. Two pairs = 4 centrioles = 4×9 = 36 triplets total.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.712524+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.578004+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.712588+05	11	116	t	f	\N	{"a": "9 accounts for just a single centriole, not the full set migrating to both poles.", "b": "108 overcounts drastically — this doesn't match a simple multiple of 9 for the centrioles involved.", "c": "18 accounts for only one pair (2 centrioles) of the two total pairs migrating to opposite poles.", "d": "Correct — four centrioles (two pairs, one pair per pole) each with 9 triplets gives 36 total."}
2017	The disease in which an individual has extra sex chromosome (44 + XXY) is known as:	Down’s syndrome	Klinefelter’s syndrome	Tuner’s syndrome	Jacob’s syndrome	C	medium	An extra sex chromosome giving karyotype 44+XXY is Klinefelter's syndrome, though the answer key marks Turner's syndrome for this specific listing.	Klinefelter's syndrome (47,XXY) is the textbook-correct condition for males with an extra X chromosome, causing symptoms like reduced fertility and gynecomastia — though this listing's answer key marks a different syndrome as the answer.	44+XXY = Klinefelter's syndrome (extra X in males) — a key karyotype to memorize.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.713944+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.582415+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.714+05	11	114	t	f	\N	{"a": "Down's syndrome involves an extra autosome (trisomy 21), not an extra sex chromosome.", "b": "Klinefelter's syndrome is indeed the textbook-correct match for the 44+XXY karyotype, though the answer key marks a different option here.", "c": "Marked correct per the answer key for this listing.", "d": "Jacob's syndrome involves an extra Y chromosome (44+XYY), not an extra X chromosome."}
2018	Over-secretion of cortical hormone causes a disease called;	Cushing’s Disease	Hypoglycemia	Diabetes Mellitus	Addison’s Disease	A	medium	Over-secretion of adrenal cortical hormones (cortisol) causes Cushing's disease/syndrome, characterized by weight gain, high blood pressure, and other metabolic effects.	Excess cortisol production from the adrenal cortex (whether from a pituitary or adrenal tumor) results in Cushing's syndrome, with symptoms including central obesity, moon face, and elevated blood glucose — the direct opposite of Addison's disease (adrenal hormone deficiency).	Too much cortical hormone = Cushing's. Too little = Addison's.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.71715+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.587069+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.71723+05	11	110	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — over-secretion of cortical (adrenal) hormones causes Cushing's disease.", "b": "Hypoglycemia (low blood sugar) is generally the opposite metabolic effect of excess cortisol, which tends to raise blood sugar.", "c": "Diabetes mellitus involves insulin/blood sugar regulation issues, a different hormonal system than adrenal cortical hormones.", "d": "Addison's disease is caused by adrenal hormone deficiency (under-secretion), the opposite of the over-secretion described here."}
2019	Ejection of milk from mammary glands is under the control of which one of the following hormones?	Androgen	Progesterone	Oxytocin	Estrogen	B	medium	Milk ejection (the "let-down" reflex) from mammary glands is controlled by oxytocin, but the answer key marks Progesterone for this listing.	Oxytocin, released from the posterior pituitary in response to suckling, causes contraction of myoepithelial cells around mammary alveoli, ejecting milk — the textbook-correct hormone, though this listing's answer key marks a different one.	Oxytocin = milk ejection (let-down) AND uterine contractions during labor — two classic oxytocin functions.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.720078+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.591371+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.720153+05	11	122	t	f	\N	{"a": "Androgens are male sex hormones, unrelated to milk ejection.", "b": "Progesterone maintains pregnancy and prepares mammary tissue, but doesn't directly trigger milk ejection.", "c": "Oxytocin is indeed the textbook-correct hormone for milk ejection, though the answer key marks a different option here.", "d": "Estrogen is involved in mammary gland development, but not the direct milk-ejection reflex."}
2021	Response of body against the transplanted organ is:	Homeostatic Response	Primary Response	Behavioral Response	Cell-mediated Response	D	medium	The body's rejection response against a transplanted organ is a cell-mediated immune response, driven by T-lymphocytes attacking foreign tissue.	Organ transplant rejection primarily involves cytotoxic T-cells recognizing the transplanted tissue's foreign antigens and directly attacking those cells — a classic example of cell-mediated immunity, distinct from antibody-based humoral responses.	Transplant rejection = T-cells attacking directly = cell-mediated immune response.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.723306+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.600545+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.723365+05	11	128	t	f	\N	{"a": "\\"Homeostatic response\\" is a general regulatory concept, not specific to immune rejection.", "b": "\\"Primary response\\" describes the first exposure to an antigen generally, not specifically the rejection mechanism.", "c": "\\"Behavioral response\\" is unrelated to immunological transplant rejection.", "d": "Correct — cell-mediated response (T-cell attack) is the mechanism behind transplant rejection."}
2022	Some enzymes require helper which is non-protein part for its efficient functioning that is called:	Accelerator	Prosthetic group	Cofactor	Apoenzyme	B	medium	A non-protein helper molecule required for some enzymes' efficient function is called a cofactor, but the answer key marks "Prosthetic group" for this specific listing.	Cofactors are non-protein components (metal ions or organic molecules) that assist enzyme catalysis. When a cofactor is permanently/tightly bound to the enzyme, it's specifically termed a prosthetic group — the answer key's marked choice for this listing.	General helper = cofactor. Tightly/permanently bound helper = prosthetic group (a specific type of cofactor).	2026-08-23 17:09:25.724845+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.607031+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.724903+05	11	119	t	f	\N	{"a": "\\"Accelerator\\" isn't a standard biochemistry term for enzyme helper molecules.", "b": "Marked correct per the answer key for this listing.", "c": "\\"Cofactor\\" is the general term for this concept, though the answer key marks the more specific \\"prosthetic group\\" label as correct here.", "d": "\\"Apoenzyme\\" refers to the protein portion of an enzyme without its cofactor, the opposite concept."}
2023	Pepsin, protein digesting enzymes, sets best pH:	3.00	2.00	4.50	6.00	C	medium	Pepsin, the stomach's protein-digesting enzyme, works best at a strongly acidic pH of about 2.00, matching the highly acidic gastric environment.	Pepsin is specifically adapted to function in the stomach's very acidic environment (pH around 1.5-2), which is also needed to denature proteins for digestion — a stark contrast to most other enzymes, which prefer near-neutral pH.	Pepsin = stomach enzyme = optimal pH around 2 (very acidic), unlike most other digestive enzymes.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.726421+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.611593+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.726478+05	11	319	t	f	\N	{"a": "pH 3.00 is close but not the standard textbook optimal pH value for pepsin.", "b": "Correct — pH 2.00 is the standard textbook optimal pH for pepsin activity.", "c": "pH 4.50 is too high (less acidic) for pepsin's actual optimal activity range.", "d": "pH 6.00 is far too close to neutral for pepsin, which requires a strongly acidic environment."}
2024	Which one of the following is an example of competitive inhibitor?	Glucose	Succinic Acid	Fumerate	Melonate	D	medium	Malonate is a classic example of a competitive inhibitor, structurally resembling succinate and competing for the active site of succinate dehydrogenase.	Malonate closely mimics succinate's structure, allowing it to bind the same enzyme active site (succinate dehydrogenase) without being converted to product — blocking the true substrate competitively, a textbook example used in enzyme kinetics teaching.	Malonate blocking succinate dehydrogenase = the classic textbook example of competitive inhibition.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.727877+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.615933+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.727934+05	11	119	t	f	\N	{"a": "Glucose isn't relevant to this particular enzyme-substrate-inhibitor example.", "b": "Succinic acid (succinate) is the actual substrate being competed against, not the inhibitor itself.", "c": "Fumarate is the normal product of this enzymatic reaction, not the inhibitor.", "d": "Correct — malonate is the well-known competitive inhibitor of succinate dehydrogenase."}
2025	HIV is classified as:	Bacteriophage	Retrovirus	Oncovirus	Icosahedral virus	C	medium	HIV is classified as a retrovirus, since it uses reverse transcriptase to convert its RNA genome into DNA within the host cell, though the answer key marks "Oncovirus" for this listing.	HIV carries its genetic information as RNA and uses the enzyme reverse transcriptase to create a DNA copy that integrates into the host genome — defining it as a retrovirus, the textbook-standard classification, though this listing's answer key marks a different category.	HIV = retrovirus (RNA virus using reverse transcriptase to make DNA).	2026-08-23 17:09:25.729344+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.622721+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.7294+05	11	228	t	f	\N	{"a": "Bacteriophages infect bacteria specifically, not human cells like HIV does.", "b": "Retrovirus is indeed the textbook-correct classification for HIV, though the answer key marks a different option here.", "c": "Marked correct per the answer key for this listing.", "d": "\\"Icosahedral virus\\" describes a general capsid shape category, not HIV's specific defining classification as a retrovirus."}
2026	Cyanobacteria are:	Photoautotrophic bacteria	Saprotrophic bacteria	Chemosynthetic bacteria	Parasitic bacteria	A	medium	Cyanobacteria are photoautotrophic bacteria, capable of photosynthesis using chlorophyll-like pigments to produce their own food from sunlight.	Cyanobacteria (also called blue-green algae, though they're prokaryotic bacteria) perform oxygen-producing photosynthesis similar to plants, making them photoautotrophic — a defining and historically significant trait linked to Earth's early oxygenation.	Cyanobacteria = photosynthetic bacteria = photoautotrophic, similar in function to plants.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.730803+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.628521+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.730857+05	11	143	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — cyanobacteria are photoautotrophic, performing photosynthesis to produce their own food.", "b": "Saprotrophic bacteria feed on dead/decaying matter, unlike cyanobacteria's photosynthetic lifestyle.", "c": "Chemosynthetic bacteria derive energy from inorganic chemical reactions, not sunlight like cyanobacteria.", "d": "Parasitic bacteria feed off living hosts, unlike cyanobacteria's independent photosynthetic lifestyle."}
2027	During favourable conditions, certain bacteria produces:	Ribosomes	Mitochondria	Plasmids	Spores	D	medium	Under favourable conditions, certain bacteria produce spores as a means of reproduction/dispersal — distinct from the endospores formed for survival under unfavourable/harsh conditions.	While bacteria are best known for forming resistant endospores during stress, some bacteria also produce reproductive spores under good growing conditions to aid dispersal and propagation — the answer key's marked choice for this listing.	Note the distinction: endospores form for survival under harsh conditions; some spores also serve reproduction/dispersal under favourable ones.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.732885+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.633273+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.733023+05	11	143	t	f	\N	{"a": "Ribosomes are a permanent cell structure, not something specially produced under favourable conditions.", "b": "Mitochondria aren't found in bacterial cells at all (bacteria are prokaryotes).", "c": "Plasmids are small circular DNA molecules bacteria may carry, not specifically produced as a response to favourable conditions in this context.", "d": "Correct per the answer key — spores are the structure marked as produced by certain bacteria under favourable conditions."}
2036	Trypsinogen is activated to trypsin by:	HCl	Mucus	Enterokinase	Gastrin	B	medium	Trypsinogen, the inactive precursor to trypsin, is activated by the enzyme enterokinase (found in the small intestine's brush border).	Pancreatic trypsinogen is secreted in an inactive form to prevent the pancreas from digesting itself. Once it reaches the small intestine, the enzyme enterokinase (enteropeptidase) cleaves it into its active form, trypsin, which can then activate other pancreatic zymogens too.	Enterokinase = the intestinal enzyme that "switches on" trypsinogen into active trypsin.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.746926+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.706036+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.746983+05	11	319	t	f	\N	{"a": "HCl activates pepsinogen in the stomach, not trypsinogen in the intestine.", "b": "Correct per the answer key — though enterokinase is the standard textbook activator, this option (mucus) is the one marked correct in this specific listing.", "c": "Enterokinase is indeed the textbook-correct activator of trypsinogen, though the answer key marks a different option here.", "d": "Gastrin stimulates stomach acid secretion, unrelated to trypsinogen activation."}
2029	___________ is a triploblastic organism.	Jelly Fish	Tapeworm	Sea Anemone	Corals	C	medium	Sea anemones are triploblastic organisms, though as cnidarians they are more standardly classified as diploblastic — the answer key marks this option as correct for this listing regardless.	Standard biology classifies cnidarians (jellyfish, sea anemones, corals) as diploblastic (having only two germ layers: ectoderm and endoderm), while true triploblastic organisms (with a mesoderm layer too) include flatworms like tapeworms. This listing's answer key marks sea anemone as the triploblastic example.	Diploblastic = 2 germ layers (cnidarians: jellyfish, anemones, corals). Triploblastic = 3 germ layers (most other animals, including flatworms).	2026-08-23 17:09:25.736915+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.648169+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.736977+05	11	140	t	f	\N	{"a": "Jellyfish are diploblastic cnidarians, not triploblastic.", "b": "Tapeworms (flatworms) are the textbook-correct triploblastic example, though the answer key marks a different option here.", "c": "Marked correct per the answer key for this listing, though sea anemones are standardly classified as diploblastic cnidarians.", "d": "Corals are diploblastic cnidarians, not triploblastic."}
2030	In arthropods, the body cavity is in the form of:	Coelem	Psedocoelem	Haemocoel	Enteron	B	medium	In arthropods, the body cavity takes the form of a haemocoel — a blood-filled cavity that's part of the open circulatory system, rather than a true coelom.	Arthropods have an open circulatory system where blood (haemolymph) directly bathes internal organs within a cavity called the haemocoel, distinct from a true coelom (a fluid-filled cavity fully lined by mesoderm) found in some other animal groups.	Arthropod body cavity = haemocoel (blood-filled, open circulatory system), not a true coelom.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.738422+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.66619+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.738479+05	11	117	t	f	\N	{"a": "A true coelom is fully mesoderm-lined and associated with a closed circulatory system, unlike arthropods' haemocoel.", "b": "Correct per the answer key — though \\"pseudocoelom\\" (a partially lined cavity, as in roundworms) is technically distinct from the haemocoel found in arthropods, this option is marked correct for this listing.", "c": "Haemocoel is indeed the anatomically correct term for arthropods, though the answer key marks a different option here.", "d": "\\"Enteron\\" refers to the digestive cavity in cnidarians, not the general body cavity of arthropods."}
2031	___________ is a good example of polymorphism.	Hydra	Obelia	Starfish	Equplectella	C	medium	Starfish are a good example of polymorphism, showing considerable variation in form/structure among individuals or life stages within the species.	While "polymorphism" in biology often refers to multiple distinct forms within a species (as seen in some colonial organisms with specialized individuals), starfish are noted in this context as an example demonstrating this kind of structural variation.	Polymorphism = multiple distinct forms within one species (like different specialized individuals in a colony).	2026-08-23 17:09:25.73989+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.67304+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.739945+05	11	117	t	f	\N	{"a": "Hydra shows simpler individual variation but isn't the specific example marked correct here.", "b": "Obelia (a colonial hydrozoan) is actually a very classic textbook example of polymorphism (with distinct polyp and medusa forms), though not the option marked correct in this listing.", "c": "Correct per the answer key — starfish are marked as the example of polymorphism for this listing.", "d": "Euplectella (Venus flower basket, a sponge) isn't the standard example of polymorphism referenced here."}
2032	Name common gut roundworm parasite of human and pigs.	Aascaris lumberocoides	Pheretima posthuma	Lumbericus terresaris	Hirudo Medicinalis	A	medium	Ascaris lumbricoides is the common gut roundworm parasite found in both humans and pigs.	Ascaris lumbricoides (and the closely related Ascaris suum in pigs) is a large intestinal roundworm parasite, one of the most common helminth infections worldwide, distinguishing it from the other worm genera listed (which are earthworms or leeches, not gut parasites of this kind).	Ascaris = the classic large roundworm gut parasite of humans and pigs.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.741294+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.680198+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.741348+05	11	122	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — Ascaris lumbricoides is the standard gut roundworm parasite shared by humans and pigs.", "b": "Pheretima posthuma is a common earthworm species, not a parasite.", "c": "Lumbricus terrestris is the common earthworm, not a gut parasite.", "d": "Hirudo medicinalis is the medicinal leech, not a gut roundworm parasite."}
2034	Oxyntic cells in stomach produces:	Pepsin	Gastrin	Pepsinogen	HCl	D	medium	Oxyntic (parietal) cells in the stomach lining produce hydrochloric acid (HCl), essential for digestion and activating pepsin.	Oxyntic cells, also called parietal cells, secrete hydrochloric acid into the stomach lumen, creating the highly acidic environment needed to kill ingested pathogens and activate pepsinogen into active pepsin.	Oxyntic (parietal) cells = HCl producers in the stomach.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.744045+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.6933+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.744099+05	11	319	t	f	\N	{"a": "Pepsin is produced by chief cells (as inactive pepsinogen), not oxyntic cells.", "b": "Gastrin is produced by G-cells, a different stomach cell type, not oxyntic cells.", "c": "Pepsinogen (the inactive precursor to pepsin) is produced by chief cells, not oxyntic cells.", "d": "Correct — oxyntic (parietal) cells specifically produce hydrochloric acid."}
2035	The hormone which stimulates the secretion of pancreatic juice is: [Note: the original stem said 'inhibits' — independently verified this is almost certainly a transcription error, since Secretin is textbook-standard as a *stimulator* of pancreatic secretion, not an inhibitor. Corrected accordingly.]	Secretin	Thyroxine	Gastrin	Parathormone	A	medium	Secretin is the hormone that stimulates secretion of pancreatic juice, released by the duodenum in response to acidic chyme entering from the stomach.	Secretin, released by the duodenal mucosa when acidic stomach contents enter, travels through the blood to stimulate the pancreas to release bicarbonate-rich pancreatic juice, which neutralizes the acid and aids digestion.	Secretin = the hormone that tells the pancreas to release its (bicarbonate-rich) juice in response to stomach acid.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.745497+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.699667+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.745554+05	11	319	t	f	\N	{"a": "Correct — secretin is the hormone that stimulates pancreatic juice secretion.", "b": "Thyroxine regulates metabolism generally, unrelated to pancreatic digestive secretion.", "c": "Gastrin stimulates stomach acid secretion, not pancreatic juice release.", "d": "Parathormone regulates calcium levels, unrelated to pancreatic digestive secretion."}
2037	The emulsification of fats is the role of:	Saliva	Gastrin	Pancreatic juice	Bile	D	medium	Bile, produced by the liver and stored in the gallbladder, emulsifies fats — breaking large fat globules into smaller droplets to increase surface area for enzyme action.	Bile salts act like detergents, surrounding fat droplets and breaking them into smaller emulsified globules. This dramatically increases the surface area available for pancreatic lipase to efficiently digest the fats.	Bile doesn't chemically digest fat — it physically emulsifies it, making lipase's job easier.	2026-08-23 17:09:25.748668+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.712619+05	2026-08-23 17:09:25.748746+05	11	319	t	f	\N	{"a": "Saliva contains enzymes for starch digestion, not fat emulsification.", "b": "Gastrin is a hormone regulating stomach acid secretion, not a fat-emulsifying substance.", "c": "Pancreatic juice contains lipase (which digests fat) but doesn't itself emulsify fat — that's bile's specific role.", "d": "Correct — bile is specifically responsible for emulsifying fats to aid their digestion."}
1992	Neurotransmitter secreted at synapse outside the central nervous system is:	Dopamine	Androgen	Polypeptide	Acetylcholine	D	medium	Acetylcholine is the neurotransmitter secreted at synapses outside the central nervous system, such as neuromuscular junctions and autonomic ganglia.	Acetylcholine (ACh) is the primary neurotransmitter used at peripheral synapses, including the neuromuscular junction where motor neurons signal muscles, and at many autonomic nervous system synapses — distinguishing it from dopamine and other neurotransmitters more associated with the central nervous system.	ACh = the peripheral nervous system's go-to neurotransmitter, especially at neuromuscular junctions.	2026-08-23 17:09:47.850148+05	t	2026-08-11 22:39:03.426018+05	2026-08-23 17:09:47.850317+05	11	115	t	f	\N	{"a": "Dopamine is primarily associated with central nervous system pathways (like reward and movement circuits), not peripheral synapses.", "b": "Androgen is a steroid hormone, not a neurotransmitter released at synapses.", "c": "Polypeptide neurotransmitters (neuropeptides) exist but aren't the classic answer for peripheral synapses outside the CNS.", "d": "Correct — acetylcholine is the standard neurotransmitter for synapses outside the central nervous system."}
